# MADS

Mesh adaptive direct search (MADS) is a local derivative-free optimizer that
tests directions around an incumbent on an adaptive mesh. Basin implements
OrthoMADS, which generates orthogonal poll directions deterministically. The
method needs no interpolating objective model and can handle nonsmooth
functions.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/mads/struct.Mads.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                             |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A scalar function, including nonsmooth functions          |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`; nonlinear values in constrained mode      |
  | Search      | Local and deterministic, from one starting point          |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained, box-only, or nonlinear-inequality-only mode |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`              |

## When to use it

Consider MADS when derivatives are unavailable and nonsmoothness makes a local
linear or quadratic model unreliable. For a smooth objective, compare
[NEWUOA](../newuoa/) or [BOBYQA](../bobyqa/), which use quadratic models to
capture curvature. MADS polls directions explicitly, so its evaluation cost
can grow quickly with the number of parameters.

[Audet and Dennis (2006)](#ref-audet-dennis-2006) establish the MADS framework;
read it with the [2008 erratum](#ref-audet-custodio-dennis-2008).
[Abramson et al. (2009)](#ref-abramson-audet-dennis-le-digabel-2009) introduce
the deterministic OrthoMADS directions used here.

The nonsmooth convergence results concern refining subsequences and their
limit points under regularity and direction-density assumptions. Clarke
stationarity requires appropriate local Lipschitz assumptions; constrained
results also depend on constraint qualifications. Those results do not imply
global optimality or certify a finite run. Although the implementation can
evaluate discontinuous objectives, the same stationarity conclusion does not
automatically extend to an arbitrary discontinuous function.

## How it works

Each iteration constructs an orthogonal integer basis using a Halton sequence
and a scaled Householder reflection. The positive and negative columns form
`2n` candidate poll directions for `n` parameters. Basin polls opportunistically:
an improvement can end the poll before all candidates have been evaluated.

Trial points lie on a mesh around the incumbent. An improving poll coarsens
the mesh, and an unsuccessful poll refines it. The fine mesh size shrinks
faster than the poll size, making increasingly varied directions available as
the search refines. The Halton schedule chooses the directions without a
random seed.

The default mode accepts objective improvements. Box-bounded mode rejects
infeasible probes without evaluating their objective. The progressive-barrier
mode instead tracks objective and aggregate constraint violation, retaining
feasible and infeasible search histories.

## Example

This nonsmooth absolute-value valley has its unique minimum at `[1.0, 2.0]`.
The example sets the minimum poll size on the solver and the evaluation budget
on the executor.

<!-- compile: solver-mads -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, Mads, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct AbsValley;

impl CostFunction for AbsValley {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).abs() + 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0).abs())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = Mads::new()
        .with_initial_poll_size(1.0)
        .with_minimum_poll_size(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(AbsValley, solver, vec![0.0, 0.0])
        .max_cost_evals(5_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_param()[1] - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Choose the mode that matches your constraints

`Mads::new()` is unconstrained and ignores any constraint declaration on the
problem. Select a constrained mode explicitly to enforce constraints.

  | Mode                             | Complete constraint declaration                   | Behavior                                                      |
  | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | `Mads::new()`                     | None required                                     | Accepts objective improvements                                |
  | `Mads::new().bounded()`           | Box bounds only                                   | Clamps the start, then rejects out-of-box probes               |
  | `Mads::new().constrained()`       | Nonlinear inequalities only                       | Allows an infeasible start and uses a progressive barrier      |

The bounded mode uses an extreme barrier: a point outside the box is treated
as having infinite cost without calling the objective. Declare bounds with
`ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(lower, upper))` and attach
them to the problem with `Constrained::new`.

The constrained mode implements the progressive-barrier approach of
[Audet and Dennis (2009)](#ref-audet-dennis-2009). It aggregates residuals as
`h(x) = sum(max(c[j](x), 0)^2)` and progressively lowers an admissible violation
threshold. It can explore around both feasible and infeasible incumbents.
The current mode requires a nonlinear-inequality-only declaration and the
`NonlinearInequalityValues` callback. It does not accept additional bounds,
linear blocks, or nonlinear equalities. Compare [COBYLA](../cobyla/) for a
problem that combines those blocks.

### Check the progressive barrier's selected result

This example starts outside the unit disk and uses the constrained mode. The
unconstrained minimum is `[2.0, 0.0]`; the constrained minimum is `[1.0, 0.0]`.

<!-- compile: solver-mads-constrained -->
```rust
use basin::{
    Constrained, ConstraintSet, ConstraintSolveError, CostFunction, Executor,
    Mads, NonlinearInequalityValues, ViolationMeasure,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct DiskQuadratic;

impl CostFunction for DiskQuadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 2.0).powi(2) + x[1].powi(2))
    }
}

impl NonlinearInequalityValues for DiskQuadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Values = Vec<f64>;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn nonlinear_inequalities(
        &self,
        x: &Self::Param,
    ) -> Result<Self::Values, ConstraintSolveError<Self::Error>> {
        Ok(vec![x[0].powi(2) + x[1].powi(2) - 1.0])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let constraints = ConstraintSet::new().with_nonlinear_inequalities(1);
    let problem = Constrained::new(DiskQuadratic, constraints);
    let solver = Mads::new()
        .with_initial_poll_size(1.0)
        .with_minimum_poll_size(1e-7)
        .constrained();

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![2.0, 0.0])
        .max_cost_evals(5_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    let (x, cost, violation) = result.state.best().unwrap();
    assert_eq!(violation.measure(), &ViolationMeasure::SquaredL2);
    assert!(violation.value < 1e-10);
    assert!(x[0].powi(2) + x[1].powi(2) <= 1.0 + 1e-5);
    assert!((cost - 1.0).abs() < 1e-3);
}
```

The violation report contains the squared-sum measure, not the largest
constraint residual. Inspect the selected point and individual constraints
before accepting it: reaching the poll-size floor does not certify
feasibility. Selection can increase the objective as feasibility improves,
so objective-only executor targets and stall checks are unavailable in this
mode.

Return finite constraint values. The current positive-part aggregation skips
NaN residuals, so a NaN must not be used to represent an infeasible evaluation.
Report callback failures through `ConstraintSolveError::Evaluation` instead.

### Scale the poll sizes and allow enough evaluations

The initial poll size defaults to `1.0`, and the minimum defaults to `1e-6`.
Choose positive values with the minimum below the initial size. Rescale
parameters so a common poll scale makes sense across coordinates. A small
minimum poll size controls geometric resolution; it does not bound parameter
error or establish stationarity in a finite run.

`with_absolute_poll_size_tolerance` adds an optional earlier stopping check
without changing the mesh schedule. A failed poll can require `2n` objective
evaluations in the unconstrained mode. Constrained iterations may poll around
more than one center. Keep an evaluation budget on the executor, and budget
nonlinear callbacks separately with
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::Residual, limit)` when needed.

## Implementation

Unconstrained and box-bounded MADS publish their incumbent through
`PointState`. Progressive-barrier MADS uses `SelectedState` with an unscaled
`SquaredL2` violation report and identity transformation. Constraint calls
count as residual evaluations; box-rejected probes make no objective call.

The solver owns the mesh, Halton counters, and barrier history. Use
`Executor::run_with_solver` to retain it and inspect `poll_size()` and
`mesh_index()`. Fresh initialization resets those histories and reevaluates
the starting point. An `ExactCheckpoint` retains them for exact continuation.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`, in every
mode. Poll geometry uses internal integer and floating-point buffers without
matrix capabilities, a BLAS/LAPACK provider, or an RNG. The implementation is
deterministic and works on WASM.

